The development of Polymer Electrolyte Membranes (PEMs)with high proton conductivity and good mechanical flexibility is one of the critical requisites towards the performance enhancement of the Proton Exchange Membrane (PEM) fuel cell. Notably, till date, Nafion? and Nafion? based composite polymers have shown prominent proton conducting ability（ca. $2\times10^{-1}\ \mathsf{S}\quad\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ at moderate temperature $(60-80^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and high relative humidity $(98\%$ RH).[l As a result, these materials are the frontrunners as proton exchange membranes in the existing low-temperature PEM based fuel cells. However, the lack of thorough understanding in structure-propertyrelationshipof these amorphous polymers/polymer-based composites is a barrier for their further developments[1d, 2b] On the contrary, a recent category of crystalline porous polymers, known as covalent organic frameworks (COFs)[2-5] has been tested as proton conducting materials owing to their well-defined porous crystalline structure and tailorable chemical properties. When employed in a PEM fuel cell, the ordered nano-pores are expected to enhance the proton mobility and improve the proton carrier holding capacity within the pores, thereby leading to high proton conductivity.8b It is noteworthy that a few porous materials like Metal Organic Frameworks (MOFs), and COFs with loaded career molecules have been tested for proton conduction.[6-9] However, the poor mechanical flexibility， low crystallinity and disrupted pore channels (because of the high pressure employed for pellet making[6a8cl) has often resulted in inferior proton conductivityand subsequently, in poor performance under the real PEMFC operating conditions. Therefore, the immediate challenge is to engineer a self-standing flexible membrane consisting of crystalline COFparticles  without losingits structural characteristics such as crystallinity and porosity.
Keeping this in perspective, herein we present a strategic approach to synthesize a series of self-standing, flexible porous covalent organic framework membranes (COFMs） as proton reservoirs and to allow their subsequent utilization in PEM fuel cells. In the present study, we have fabricated three COFs $[{\mathsf{T}}{\mathsf{p B D}}({\mathsf{M e}})_{2}$ ，TpAzo and TpBpy] via amine— $p$ -toluene sulfonic acid $(\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O})$ salt mediated, slow-crystallization approach[10] into three self-standing, flexible porous COFMs.Herein $p$ -toluene sulfonic acid bestows two advantages: First of all, it serves as a co-reagent during the baking process, resulting in an extensive increment in the crystallinity as well as the porosity of the COFMs.l Secondly, the entrapment of this efficient proton carrier $(\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O})$ ，allows for proton conducting ability into the resulting self-standing membranes. lt is important to note that although the impregnation of proton donors $(P T S A.H_{2}O)$ into these COFMs is smaller $(8\cdot12\ \mathsf{w t.\%})$ compared to other reported materials[8bl the membranes still display remarkably high proton conducting performance. The proton conductivity reported here is superior to all the conductive COFs and is measured as $5.3\times10^{-}$ $^{2}\cdot7.8\times10^{-2}\textcircled{<}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ respectively at $80^{\circ}$ Cunder $95\%$ relative humidity.
The methodology of synthesizing COFs and their simultaneous fabrication into flexible self-standing COFMs [(elaborately discussed in the Supporting Information (Sl)], involves the slow baking of symmetrical organic linkers in the presence of $p$ -toluene sulfonic acid $(\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O})$ and water at moderate temperature $(50-90^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ for 3-4 days. The formation of the $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D}(\mathsf{M e})_{2}$ ， PTSA@TpAzo and PTSA@TpBpy COFMs,by this synthetic route,is accompanied by impregnation of proton carriers viz.,PTSA.HzO molecules within the COFM pores (Figure 1). The formation of the COF skeleton is verified using PXRD analyses (Figure 1e). Interestingly, in all the cases, the experimental PXRD patterns of the as-synthesised materials primarily correspond to the simulated PXRD patterns of the crystalline $\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O}$ molecules. However, the formation of COFMs could also be recognized by the presence of the low intense peaks[4,14] positioned at $\ 2\theta=3.1$ to $5.3^{\circ}$ The uncovering of the COFMs is further attempted by removing the water-soluble PTSA using hot water treatment. A marked enhancement of the intensity of the COF peaks confirms their formation and the loading of the $\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O}$ molecules within the COF pores (Figures1e and S3, Sl). The experimental PXRD patterns for all
