the three COFs are observed to be in complete agreement with the simulated patterns of eclipsed (AA) stacking models15 (Figure S1).
The FT-IR spectra of $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D(M e)}_{2}$ includes the signature bands of both PTSA and COFM TpBD(Me)z. The band at $3407~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ indicate the presence of $-0-H$ functionality of the PTSA while the bands at 1108, and 1035 and 671 cm1 correspond to the stretching of $-\mathsf{S}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{O}$ and $-\mathsf{S}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}$ bonds of the impregnated PTSA inside the nano-pores of the ${\mathsf{T p B D}}({\mathsf{M e}})_{2}$ membrane.[16] A similar validation could be derived from the FT-IR spectra of the other two COFMs as well (Section S-4, SI in details).
The surface area measurements of the $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D(M e)}_{2}$ PTSA@TpAzo and PTSA@TpBpy membranes at 77K show a low surface area ranging between $10{-}29\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Interestingly, after the removal of the PTSA molecules via hot water treatment, the COF membranes divulge a very high internal surface area (2583- $2933\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ .The pore size distribution calculated using the DFT method indicates the presence of only one type of pore $(28\mathrm{~\AA~}$ 25A, 24A pore diameter for TpAzo, TpBpy, TpBD $(M{\mathsf{e}})_{2}$ COFMs respectively) throughout the frameworks (Figure S5). This feature notably underlines the PTSA holding capacity of the COFs inside the highly ordered pore channels without any structural alterations (Figure 2j).
Further, the TGA carried out on the as-synthesized COFMs reveals a PTSA loading of 12,10 and 8 wt $\%$ in case of PTSA@TpAzo，PTSA@TpBpyand $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D(M e)}_{2}$ respectively (Figure 2k). It is noteworthy that the amount of the carrier loading is significantly low in comparison to the reports on ex-situ loaded proton carriers.[8b] A careful investigation of the TGA curves implies that although the thermal release of the trapped $p_{T S A.H_{2}O}$ molecules from the pores initiates at $102^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ， the removal of the carriers only completes at $190^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .This study therefore hints at the thermal stability of the PTSA@COFMs between $25-180^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ， making them ideal for high-temperature proton conduction studies.
The proton conductivity of the PTSA@COFMs is measured at different temperatures (from $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ）under $95\%$ RH (Figure 3). The conductivity study reveals that, among all COFMs PTSA@TpAzo shows the highest proton conducting ability of 6.3 $\textsf{X}10^{-2}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under $95\%$ RH.The conductivity increases with the increase of temperature and reaches to a maximum of $7.8\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ，which is the highest among all the crystalline porous organic polymers (Table T7, Sl) based proton conducting membranes reported to date.[2b,17] A similar trend is alsoobservedinthecaseofPTSA@TpBpyand $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D(M e)}_{2}$ membranes, which show the maximum proton conductivity of $6.2\times10^{-2}$ and $5.3\times10^{-2}\mathsf{S c m}^{-1}$ respectively at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under $95\%$ RH. The proton conductivity of ${\mathsf{T p B D}}({\mathsf{M e}})_{2}$ ，TpAzo and TpBpy membranes was further tested after removal of the PTSA.The washed COFMs show very low proton conductivity $(3.4\times10^{-5}$ 。 $3.9\times10^{-5}$ and $6.6\times10^{-6}5c m^{-1}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under $95\%$ RH for TpAzo, TpBpy and ${\mathsf{T p B D}}({\mathsf{M e}})_{2}$ COFMs respectively) compared to the proton carrier impregnated COFMs of the $p{\star}$ toluene sulfonic acid. To check the impact of the in-situ impregnation approach in the crystaline framework used in the present study, a similar amount of $P T S A.H_{2}O$ (i.e., $10{-}15~\mathsf{w t.\%})$ was deliberately mixed into the plain TpAzo polymer (amorphous) (Figure S12), following which its proton conductivity was measured (Figure S19). Interestingly, this $_{e x}$ -situ PTSA loaded amorphous polymer showed inferior proton conducting ability (8.4 $\textsf{X}10^{-6}$ and $1.5\times10^{-4}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ temperature respectively under $95\%$ RH) as compared to its crystalline, in-situ counterpart. In another study, first a highly crystalline porous (after removal of $\mathsf{P T S A.H_{2}O})$ TpAzo COFM was dipped into a saturated aqueous solution of PTSA for 24 hours. Then, the percentage of ex-situ loading of proton carriers within the membrane and its subsequent proton conducting ability was investigated. It was found that despite of higher PTSA loading
