(22 wt $\%$ ）(Figure S6), the proton conducting ability of the ex-situ loaded TpAzo COFM (Figure S20)is less than the in-situ PTSA loaded TpAzo COFM. The poor performance of the ex-situ PTSA $H_{2}O$ loaded TpAzo COFM is believed to be a result of the unorganised filling of the carrier molecules within the pores of the COF crystalites, against a regular entrapment of the proton carriers within the growing COF matrix, as in the case of the insitu loaded COFMs. This outcome also substantiates the need to utilize the proposed salt mediated, slow crystallization techniques for constructing COFMs with uniform and long-range ordered pores and alongside.[18]
The thickness of a proton conducting membrane is a crucial factor to decide its performance under real fuel cell operating conditions. Therefore, in the present study, the thickness of the PTSA@COFMs is controlled by varying the thickness of the moulds (Section S-2, Sl). The thickness of the COFMs could be varied between $100{-}300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ，which could be verified from the cross-section study of the SEM images of PTSA@TpBD(Me)2, PTSA@TpAzo and PTSA@TpBpy respectively (Figures 2d-f). The surface of the PTSA@COFMs was found to be smooth and devoid of any cracks (Figures 2g-i, Figure S7). The proton conductivity was found to further increase upon decreasing the thickness of the in-situ PTSA loaded COFMs (Figures 3d-f), which suggests the potential shortening of the mean free path for the diffusion of protons. The dynamic mechanical analysis of PTSA@TpAzo COFM reveals its competent viscoelastic nature with a breaking strain of $5.4\%$ ，(Figures S9 and S10) which underpins the highly flexible behavior of the membrane.
Further to determine the proton conducting mechanism, the activation energy of the PTSA@COFMs was determined using temperature dependent conductivity profiles. The activation energy was found (Figures 3g-i) to range between 0.11 to 0.23 eV, which suggests the presence of a Grotthuss type mechanism.[6a.12] The material (PTSA@TpAzo COFM) shows excellent proton conductivity even after multiple cycles (tested up to three cycles) (Figure S14). Furthermore, a system level validation of the proton conducting ability of the PTSA@COFMs has been performed by assembling and testing a single cell of the PEMFC.Herein,a membrane electrode assembly (MEA)was fabricated using COFM as the proton exchange membrane. The anode and the cathode layers were made up of 40 wt.% Pt/C catalyst, where the Pt loading had been maintained as $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ on each electrode. The single cell assembly displays an open circuitvoltage（OCV）of $0.81~\mathrm{~v~}$ ，indicating that the cell configuration is efficient at maintaining the respective electrode processes (Figure 4a).The steady OCV also substantiates the ability of the COFMs to prevent any crossover of the reactant gases.Normally, ${\sf H}_{2}$ -crossover results in drop in the OCV and the steady OCV obtained here confirms that COFM is impermeable to molecular hydrogen $^{[13]}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{n}}$ polarization, the COFM based MEA delivers a maximum power output of $24\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (at 0.33V) and a maximum current density of $90~\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (at 0.20 V) (Figure 4b). It is to be noted that no back pressure was being applied throughout the study. Thus, this study in the fuel-cell polarization mode validates the functioning of the single cell assembled using the COFMs. The obtained power density is notably the highest among the reported crystalline porous polymeric materials (MOFs and COFs)besides being comparable to the reported crystalline composite membranes for PEMFCs till date.[8a,19]
In summary， for the first time, highly crystalline COFs interwoven into self-standing， flexible membranes have been employed as proton conducting membranes in low-temperature fuel cell under real operating conditions. The study reports the synthesis of three different PTSA@COFMs [PTSA@TpAzo, PTSA@TpBpy， and $\mathsf{P T S A@T p B D(M e)_{2}}\big]$ with three variable thicknesses( $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ， $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ， $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ ，which have been evaluated for their temperature-dependent proton conducting ability from $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ at $95\%$ RH. It was observed that the
